// Class 8 Science practice bank, part 1: Exploring the Investigative World of Science to Particulate Nature of Matter (Curiosity).
StudyData.addPractice(8, "science", {
  "investigative-world-of-science": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The factor that you measure to see the effect of a change in an experiment is the", options: ["independent variable", "control", "dependent variable", "hypothesis"], answer: 2, explain: "The dependent variable is the one that is measured. The independent variable is the factor deliberately changed.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which is the correct order of the first steps of an investigation?", options: ["Question → observe → hypothesis → experiment", "Observe → question → hypothesis → experiment", "Hypothesis → observe → question → experiment", "Experiment → hypothesis → question → observe"], answer: 1, explain: "An investigation begins with an observation, which raises a question. A hypothesis is then made and tested by an experiment.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Readings are written in tables and drawn as graphs mainly to", options: ["show the results so that patterns are easy to see", "hide the readings that look odd", "replace the experiment", "decide the conclusion in advance"], answer: 0, explain: "Tables and graphs organise the data so that patterns can be seen. Readings should never be hidden.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The average of the readings 6, 8, 10 and 12 is", options: ["36", "10", "9", "8"], answer: 2, explain: "Sum = 6 + 8 + 10 + 12 = 36. Average = 36 ÷ 4 = 9. The number 36 is only the sum.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Scientists publish and share their findings so that", options: ["no one else can use them", "the work cannot be questioned", "only one person gets credit", "others can repeat the work and check it"], answer: 3, explain: "Sharing allows other scientists to repeat the experiment and check the results, which makes science reliable.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Meena wants to find out whether the amount of water affects the growth of bean seedlings. Which should she keep the same in all pots?", options: ["The amount of water", "The type of seed, soil, pot size and sunlight", "The height of the seedlings after a week", "Nothing needs to be kept the same"], answer: 1, explain: "Only the amount of water is changed. Everything else must be kept the same so the test is fair.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Sana times how long a fixed mass of sugar takes to dissolve in 100 mL of water at 20 °C, 40 °C and 60 °C. What is the dependent variable?", options: ["The temperature of the water", "The mass of the sugar", "The time taken to dissolve", "The volume of the water"], answer: 2, explain: "Temperature is changed (independent variable). The time taken is what is measured, so it is the dependent variable.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Karan finds that a plant near a window grows taller than a plant kept in a dark cupboard, and says light is the only reason.\nWhy is his conclusion not fully reliable?", options: ["Light has no effect on plants", "Tall plants are always healthier", "Cupboards are always warmer than rooms", "He did not make sure that the plant type, soil and water were the same, so it may not be a fair test"], answer: 3, explain: "If other factors also differ, we cannot be sure that light caused the difference. A fair test changes only one factor.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): Taking the average of several readings reduces the effect of chance errors.\nReason (R): Chance errors are sometimes too high and sometimes too low, so they tend to cancel out.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Errors in single readings go in both directions. When readings are averaged they partly cancel, so R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Why should a student record a reading honestly even if it is not what she expected?", options: ["So that the experiment ends sooner", "So that the hypothesis always turns out right", "So that the conclusion is based on real evidence", "So that the teacher gives more marks for a bigger number"], answer: 2, explain: "A conclusion must follow from evidence. Changing readings to fit an expectation would make the conclusion false.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A student measures the time for 10 swings of a pendulum four times: 20.4 s, 20.0 s, 20.2 s and 20.6 s. The best value to report is", options: ["81.2 s", "20.4 s", "20.2 s", "20.3 s"], answer: 3, explain: "Sum = 20.4 + 20.0 + 20.2 + 20.6 = 81.2 s. Average = 81.2 ÷ 4 = 20.3 s.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A student tests whether the type of soil affects how much water drains through it in one minute. Which statement is correct?", options: ["Independent variable: type of soil. Dependent variable: volume of water drained. Kept same: amount of water poured.", "Independent variable: volume of water drained. Dependent variable: type of soil. Kept same: amount of water poured.", "Independent variable: type of soil. Dependent variable: amount of water poured. Kept same: volume drained.", "Independent variable: time. Dependent variable: type of soil. Kept same: volume drained."], answer: 0, explain: "The soil type is changed on purpose, the water that drains is measured, and the amount of water poured must be the same each time.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Two groups repeat the same experiment and get very different results. What is the best response?", options: ["Ignore the second group", "Check how each group carried out the test, and repeat it carefully", "Decide that one group is lying", "Throw away both results and stop"], answer: 1, explain: "Differences may come from changes in method, instruments or conditions. Checking and repeating the test is the scientific way to settle it.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A shopkeeper says his tonic makes children taller and shows five children who grew taller after taking it. What is missing before we can accept his claim?", options: ["A comparison group of similar children who did not take the tonic", "A larger bottle of the tonic", "More advertisements", "A photograph of the five children"], answer: 0, explain: "Children grow taller with age anyway. Without a comparison (control) group we cannot tell whether the tonic had any effect.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the formula for the average of a set of readings.", a: "Average = sum of all the readings ÷ number of readings.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give one reason for repeating a measurement several times.", a: "To reduce the effect of chance errors, because the average of several readings is more reliable than a single reading.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Differentiate between the independent variable and the dependent variable. Give an example.", a: "The independent variable is the factor that is deliberately changed; the dependent variable is the factor that is measured to see the effect.\nExample: in a test of how temperature affects the time sugar takes to dissolve, temperature is independent and the time taken is dependent.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A student's result does not match her hypothesis, so she wants to change the readings. Why is this wrong? Give two points.", a: "Readings are evidence, and a conclusion must follow from evidence, so they must be recorded honestly.\nA result that does not support a hypothesis is useful: the hypothesis should be revised and tested again.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State two advantages of showing results as a graph instead of only as numbers.", a: "A graph makes patterns and trends easy to see at a glance.\nIt makes it easier to compare results and to spot a reading that does not fit the pattern.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Rina wants to find out whether stirring makes sugar dissolve faster. Plan a fair test by stating the variable, what she should measure and what she should keep the same.", a: "Variable changed: stirring (one glass stirred, one not stirred).\nMeasured: the time taken for the sugar to dissolve completely.\nKept the same: mass of sugar, volume and temperature of the water, and type of glass.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Four trials of a ball rolling down a ramp take 3.1 s, 2.9 s, 3.0 s and 3.0 s. Find the value she should report and give one reason for taking several trials.", a: "Sum = 3.1 + 2.9 + 3.0 + 3.0 = 12.0 s.\nAverage = 12.0 ÷ 4 = 3.0 s.\nSeveral trials reduce the effect of chance errors in any single timing.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: Imran places two identical glasses of water in sunlight. One is wrapped in black paper and the other in white paper. Both start at 28 °C. After 30 minutes the water in the black-paper glass is at 36 °C and the water in the white-paper glass is at 32 °C.\n(i) What is the independent variable?\n(ii) Find the rise in temperature in each glass.\n(iii) What conclusion can Imran draw? Name one thing he kept the same.", a: "(i) The colour of the paper (black or white).\n(ii) Black: 36 − 28 = 8 °C. White: 32 − 28 = 4 °C.\n(iii) The water in the black-paper glass warmed more, so black paper absorbed more heat from the sunlight than white paper. Kept the same: any one of the glasses, the amount of water, the starting temperature, the time in sunlight.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "invisible-living-world": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which part of a cell acts as its control centre?", options: ["Cell membrane", "Cytoplasm", "Nucleus", "Vacuole"], answer: 2, explain: "The nucleus controls the activities of the cell. The membrane controls what enters and leaves, and the cytoplasm is the jelly-like fluid.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A cell wall is found in", options: ["human cheek cells", "plant cells", "red blood cells", "nerve cells"], answer: 1, explain: "Plant cells have a cell wall outside the cell membrane. Animal cells such as human cells do not.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is a unicellular organism?", options: ["Paramecium", "Earthworm", "Banyan tree", "Butterfly"], answer: 0, explain: "Paramecium is made of a single cell. The earthworm, banyan tree and butterfly are multicellular.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Milk is changed into curd by the action of", options: ["viruses", "protozoa", "bacteria", "algae"], answer: 2, explain: "Bacteria turn milk into curd. This is an example of a useful microorganism.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which microorganism is used to make dough rise when bread is baked?", options: ["Amoeba", "Spirogyra", "A virus", "Yeast"], answer: 3, explain: "Yeast is a fungus that helps dough rise in baking. Amoeba is a protozoan and a virus is not even a cell.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which disease is correctly matched with its cause?", options: ["Cholera – virus", "Malaria – bacterium", "Influenza – virus", "Malaria – fungus"], answer: 2, explain: "Influenza is caused by a virus. Cholera is caused by bacteria and malaria by a protozoan.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which statement about viruses is correct?", options: ["They are made of many cells", "They multiply only inside a living host cell", "They make food by photosynthesis", "They have a cell wall like plants"], answer: 1, explain: "Viruses are not cells and cannot multiply on their own. They reproduce only inside a living host cell.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Anil looks at an onion peel under a microscope. The cells are box-like with a firm outer boundary, and none of them look green.\nWhich conclusion is correct?", options: ["The cells have a cell wall but no chloroplasts", "They are animal cells", "They have chloroplasts but no nucleus", "They are viruses"], answer: 0, explain: "The firm boundary is a cell wall, as in plant cells. The cells are not green because they have no chloroplasts.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): Viruses are called unicellular organisms.\nReason (R): Viruses can multiply only inside a host cell.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "Viruses are not cells at all, so A is false. R is a true statement about viruses.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "A student wants a total magnification of 200× and has a 10× eyepiece. Which objective should she use?", options: ["10×", "20×", "40×", "100×"], answer: 1, explain: "Total magnification = eyepiece × objective, so objective = 200 ÷ 10 = 20×.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A microscope has eyepieces of 5× and 10× and objectives of 10× and 40×. The highest total magnification possible is", options: ["50×", "200×", "450×", "400×"], answer: 3, explain: "Use the largest of each: 10 × 40 = 400×. Adding the numbers (450×) is a mistake because magnifications multiply.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which sequence correctly shows increasing levels of organisation in the human body?", options: ["Muscle cell → muscle tissue → stomach → digestive system", "Stomach → muscle cell → muscle tissue → digestive system", "Digestive system → stomach → muscle tissue → muscle cell", "Muscle tissue → muscle cell → digestive system → stomach"], answer: 0, explain: "Cells form tissues, tissues form organs and organs work together as an organ system.", level: 3 },
      { q: "After the rains a village has many people with high fever and chills. Stagnant water has collected everywhere. A doctor finds a protozoan in the blood of the patients.\nWhich disease is this and what is the best control measure?", options: ["Cholera; boil all drinking water", "Malaria; stop mosquito breeding by removing stagnant water", "Influenza; avoid crowded places", "Cholera; stop mosquito breeding by removing stagnant water"], answer: 1, explain: "Malaria is caused by a protozoan carried by the Anopheles mosquito, which breeds in stagnant water.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Why are viruses placed in a different group from bacteria, although both can cause disease?", options: ["Viruses are larger than bacteria", "Viruses can be seen without a microscope", "Bacteria are cells that can live and multiply on their own, but viruses are not cells and need a host", "Bacteria are always harmful and viruses are always useful"], answer: 2, explain: "Bacteria are single cells that live and multiply by themselves. Viruses have no cell structure and multiply only in a host cell.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the two lenses of a compound microscope.", a: "The eyepiece and the objective.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is a tissue?", a: "A group of similar cells that work together to do the same job.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between a unicellular and a multicellular organism, with one example of each.", a: "A unicellular organism is made of a single cell, for example Amoeba.\nA multicellular organism is made of many cells, for example a human being or a mango tree.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why do cells in the body differ in shape and size? Give one example.", a: "Cells differ in shape and size according to the job they do.\nExample: nerve cells are long so that they can carry messages over a distance.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Give two useful and two harmful roles of microorganisms.", a: "Useful: bacteria make curd; yeast helps in baking (some microbes decompose waste).\nHarmful: bacteria cause cholera; a virus causes influenza (a protozoan causes malaria).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A microscope has a 10× eyepiece and a 40× objective. Find the total magnification. What would it become if the 40× objective is changed to a 10× objective?", a: "Total magnification = eyepiece × objective.\nWith the 40× objective: 10 × 40 = 400×.\nWith the 10× objective: 10 × 10 = 100×.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Name the three main parts found in all cells and state the function of each.", a: "Cell membrane: controls what enters and leaves the cell.\nCytoplasm: the jelly-like fluid in which the cell parts lie.\nNucleus: the control centre that directs the activities of the cell.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: A student looks at a drop of pond water under a microscope with a 10× eyepiece and a 20× objective. She sees a tiny organism that keeps changing its shape. In another slide, a thin peel of a leaf shows green bodies inside each cell.\n(i) Find the total magnification used.\n(ii) Name the green bodies and state their function.\n(iii) Is the organism in the pond water unicellular or multicellular? Name one such organism.", a: "(i) 10 × 20 = 200×.\n(ii) Chloroplasts; they carry out photosynthesis (make food for the plant).\n(iii) Unicellular, as it is a single cell. Example: Amoeba.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "health-the-ultimate-treasure": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A balanced diet contains", options: ["only carbohydrates and proteins", "carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, fibre and water in the right amounts", "only fruits and vegetables", "sweets and water"], answer: 1, explain: "A balanced diet has all the nutrients, fibre and water in the right amounts. Leaving out any of them is not balanced.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Tuberculosis and the common cold mainly spread through the", options: ["bite of a mosquito", "contaminated water", "air", "touching books"], answer: 2, explain: "Both spread through the air when an infected person coughs or sneezes.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which disease is carried by the Anopheles mosquito?", options: ["Malaria", "Typhoid", "Cholera", "Tuberculosis"], answer: 0, explain: "Malaria is carried by the Anopheles mosquito. Typhoid and cholera spread through water and food.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which disease has been eradicated through vaccination?", options: ["Polio", "Cholera", "Malaria", "Smallpox"], answer: 3, explain: "Smallpox has been eradicated. Polio has been nearly eliminated but not completely removed.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these supports mental well-being?", options: ["Adequate sleep, play and hobbies", "Staying alone all the time", "Using tobacco to relax", "Skipping meals"], answer: 0, explain: "Sleep, play, hobbies and talking to others help mental health. Tobacco and isolation harm it.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which pair contains two diseases that spread through contaminated water or food?", options: ["Malaria and dengue", "Cold and tuberculosis", "Cholera and typhoid", "Diabetes and cancer"], answer: 2, explain: "Cholera and typhoid spread through contaminated water and food. Malaria and dengue are spread by mosquitoes and cold and TB by air.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): Antibiotics are not useful in treating influenza.\nReason (R): Influenza is caused by a virus, and antibiotics act against bacteria.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Antibiotics kill or stop the growth of bacteria. Influenza is a viral disease, so they have no effect on it.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "After heavy rain, Sunita sees water collected in old tyres and flower-pot saucers behind her house.\nWhat should she do to lower the risk of dengue and malaria?", options: ["Add more water to the containers", "Cover only the drinking water tank", "Take antibiotics every day", "Empty the containers or cover them so water does not stagnate"], answer: 3, explain: "Mosquitoes breed in stagnant water. Removing or covering it cuts down their numbers and so the spread of the diseases.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which statement about vaccines is correct?", options: ["A vaccine cures a fully developed disease in everyone", "A vaccine prepares the body's defences before infection", "A vaccine is the same as an antibiotic", "Vaccines work only against bacteria"], answer: 1, explain: "A vaccine trains the body to recognise a germ so that it can respond quickly later. It is not a cure and is different from an antibiotic.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A teacher tells her class to cover their mouths when they cough. Why is this advised?", options: ["It keeps the mouth warm", "It cures the cough", "It stops germs from spreading through the air to others", "It makes the germs die at once"], answer: 2, explain: "Coughing sends out droplets with germs. Covering the mouth reduces the spread of airborne infections.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which list contains only infectious diseases?", options: ["Cholera, typhoid, dengue", "Diabetes, malaria, cold", "Cancer, tuberculosis, cholera", "Typhoid, diabetes, dengue"], answer: 0, explain: "Cholera, typhoid and dengue are all caused by germs and can spread. Diabetes and most cancers are non-infectious.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Anita eats a balanced diet and has no illness, but she is always worried and avoids meeting friends. Is she completely healthy?", options: ["Yes, because health means only the absence of disease", "Yes, because she eats a balanced diet", "No, because health also includes mental and social well-being", "No, because she needs to take antibiotics"], answer: 2, explain: "Health includes physical, mental and social well-being, so constant worry and isolation mean that her health is not complete.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Smallpox was removed from the world after a vaccination programme. What does this best show?", options: ["Vaccines make antibiotics unnecessary", "Vaccination works only for one person at a time", "Non-infectious diseases can be prevented by vaccines", "Widespread vaccination can protect a whole community and even wipe out a disease"], answer: 3, explain: "When enough people are protected the germ cannot spread, and smallpox was eradicated this way.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which list contains only measures that help to prevent the spread of infection?", options: ["Washing hands, leaving water standing in coolers, drinking safe water", "Sharing glasses, washing hands, covering food", "Covering food, drinking untreated water, washing hands", "Washing hands, drinking safe water, covering food"], answer: 3, explain: "Washing hands, safe water and covered food all block the spread of germs. Standing water, shared glasses and untreated water help germs to spread.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a vector? Give one example.", a: "A vector is an organism that carries a disease-causing germ from one host to another. Example: the Anopheles mosquito carries malaria.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Who developed the first vaccine and for which disease?", a: "Edward Jenner developed the first vaccine, against smallpox.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between infectious and non-infectious diseases with one example of each.", a: "Infectious diseases are caused by microorganisms and can spread from person to person, for example cholera.\nNon-infectious diseases are not caused by germs that spread, for example diabetes.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name two ways in which infectious diseases spread and give one disease for each.", a: "Through air, for example tuberculosis or the common cold.\nThrough contaminated water and food, for example cholera or typhoid (also through vectors such as mosquitoes: malaria or dengue).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Write two ways of looking after mental well-being.", a: "Talking to family, friends or a counsellor when feeling worried or stressed.\nGetting enough sleep and taking part in play, yoga or hobbies; avoiding tobacco and alcohol.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why is a balanced diet important? Name its components and two other habits that keep us healthy.", a: "A balanced diet gives the body all the nutrients it needs in the right amounts.\nComponents: carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, fibre and water.\nOther habits: regular exercise and enough sleep (also clean surroundings).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how vaccination protects us from a disease. Name one disease that has been eradicated and one that has been nearly eliminated by vaccination.", a: "A vaccine introduces a harmless form or part of a germ, so the body learns to recognise it.\nIf the real germ enters later, the defence system responds quickly and the person does not fall seriously ill.\nSmallpox has been eradicated; polio has been nearly eliminated.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: After floods in a town, many people had loose motions and vomiting. Doctors found that they had cholera, which is caused by bacteria, and traced it to contaminated drinking water. The health officer asked people to boil water, cover food and wash hands.\n(i) How does cholera spread?\n(ii) Give two precautions the people should follow.\n(iii) Would antibiotics be useful against cholera? Who should decide the medicine?", a: "(i) Through contaminated water and food.\n(ii) Any two: drink boiled or safe water, keep food covered, wash hands before eating and after using the toilet.\n(iii) Yes, because cholera is caused by bacteria and antibiotics act on bacteria, but they should be taken only when a doctor prescribes them.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "electricity-magnetic-and-heating-effects": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A compass needle placed near a wire is deflected when the wire carries a current. This shows that an electric current produces a", options: ["heating effect only", "magnetic field", "sound wave", "change of colour"], answer: 1, explain: "The deflection of the compass needle shows a magnetic field around the wire. It is called the magnetic effect of current.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In the formula H = I²Rt, the letter R stands for", options: ["radius", "reading", "resistance", "range"], answer: 2, explain: "R is the resistance of the conductor, measured in ohms.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A safety switch in homes that cuts off the current automatically and can be reset is a", options: ["MCB", "fuse wire", "cell", "bulb"], answer: 0, explain: "An MCB switches off the circuit when the current is too high and can be switched on again. A fuse wire melts and has to be replaced.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these appliances works mainly because of the heating effect of current?", options: ["Electric bell", "Compass", "Scrap-lifting crane", "Electric iron"], answer: 3, explain: "An electric iron has a heating element. The bell and the crane use an electromagnet.", level: 1 },
      { q: "An electromagnet behaves like a magnet only when", options: ["it is cooled", "it is kept in sunlight", "current flows through its coil", "it is hammered"], answer: 2, explain: "An electromagnet is magnetised only while current passes through the coil. When the current stops, it loses its magnetism.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which change would make an electromagnet weaker?", options: ["Adding more turns to the coil", "Removing the soft iron core", "Increasing the current", "Using more cells in series"], answer: 1, explain: "A soft iron core strengthens the magnet. More turns and more current also increase the strength.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A wire carrying current is placed near a compass needle, which deflects. If the direction of the current is reversed, the needle", options: ["does not move", "spins round and round", "stays deflected in the same way", "deflects in the opposite direction"], answer: 3, explain: "Reversing the current reverses the direction of the magnetic field, so the deflection is reversed.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A crane in a scrapyard picks up iron junk with a large electromagnet and drops it into a truck when the operator presses a switch.\nWhat makes the junk fall?", options: ["The coil is made thicker", "The magnet is cooled", "The current is switched off, so the electromagnet loses its magnetism", "The number of turns is increased"], answer: 2, explain: "An electromagnet is a magnet only while current flows. Switching off the current releases the load.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): In an electric bell the hammer keeps striking the gong while the switch is pressed.\nReason (R): The circuit is repeatedly broken and made, so the armature moves back and forth.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "When the armature is pulled, the circuit breaks and the electromagnet loses its magnetism, so the armature springs back and the circuit is made again. This repeats rapidly.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "A heater of resistance 20 Ω carries a current of 3 A for 10 s. The heat produced is", options: ["600 J", "1800 J", "180 J", "540 J"], answer: 1, explain: "H = I²Rt = 3² × 20 × 10 = 9 × 200 = 1800 J.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The current through a heater is doubled while its resistance and the time stay the same. The heat produced becomes", options: ["2 times", "8 times", "the same", "4 times"], answer: 3, explain: "H is proportional to I². Doubling the current gives 2² = 4 times the heat.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A current of 2 A flows through a 50 Ω element for 1 minute. The heat produced is", options: ["200 J", "6000 J", "12 000 J", "100 J"], answer: 2, explain: "Time = 60 s. H = I²Rt = 2² × 50 × 60 = 4 × 3000 = 12 000 J. Using 2 × 50 × 60 or forgetting to change minutes to seconds gives wrong values.", level: 3 },
      { q: "In a house, the fuse blows every time a heater and an iron are switched on together.\nWhat is the most likely reason?", options: ["The fuse wire is too thick", "The combined current is too large, so the thin fuse wire melts and breaks the circuit", "The appliances are cold", "The fuse wire has zero resistance"], answer: 1, explain: "Two high-power appliances together draw a large current. This heats the thin fuse wire until it melts, which protects the wiring.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The copper wire used to make an electromagnet is coated with an insulating layer. Why?", options: ["So that current passes through every turn instead of jumping from one turn to the next", "So that the wire becomes heavier", "So that the iron core cools down", "So that the resistance becomes zero"], answer: 0, explain: "If turns touched without insulation, current could take a shortcut across them and the coil would act like fewer turns, giving a weaker magnet.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State the formula for the heat produced when a current flows through a conductor. Give the unit of heat.", a: "H = I²Rt. Heat is measured in joules (J).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name one use of an electromagnet.", a: "Any one: lifting scrap iron in a crane, electric bell, electric motor, relay.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why is soft iron used as the core of an electromagnet? Give two points.", a: "Soft iron becomes strongly magnetised when current flows through the coil, so the magnet is strong.\nIt loses its magnetism quickly when the current is switched off.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Write two differences between a fuse and an MCB.", a: "A fuse has a thin wire that melts and must be replaced after it works; an MCB switches off the circuit and can simply be reset.\nBoth protect the circuit from too much current, but the MCB can be used again and again.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain why the hammer of an electric bell strikes the gong repeatedly while the switch is pressed. Give two points.", a: "When the switch is pressed, current flows through the electromagnet, which pulls the iron armature so that the hammer hits the gong.\nThe movement breaks the circuit, the magnetism is lost and the armature springs back, closing the circuit again; this repeats quickly.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe a simple activity to show that a current-carrying wire produces a magnetic field.", a: "Place a compass near a straight wire connected to a cell and a switch; note the direction of the needle.\nClose the switch so that current flows: the needle deflects, showing a magnetic field around the wire.\nReverse the connections to the cell: the needle deflects the other way, which shows the field depends on the direction of the current.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A heating element of resistance 25 Ω carries a current of 2 A for 5 minutes. Calculate the heat produced.", a: "Time t = 5 × 60 = 300 s.\nH = I²Rt = 2² × 25 × 300.\nH = 4 × 7500 = 30 000 J.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: A scrapyard uses a crane with a large coil of insulated wire wound on a soft iron core. When current flows, the crane lifts a heap of iron scrap. At the truck, the operator switches off the current and the scrap falls.\n(i) Name the device that lifts the scrap.\n(ii) Give two ways to make it stronger.\n(iii) Why does the scrap fall when the current is switched off?", a: "(i) An electromagnet.\n(ii) Any two: increase the number of turns of the coil, increase the current, use a soft iron core.\n(iii) The coil is a magnet only while current flows, so on switching off it loses its magnetism and no longer holds the scrap.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "exploring-forces": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A force can change", options: ["only the colour of an object", "only the mass of an object", "the speed, direction or shape of an object", "only the temperature of an object"], answer: 2, explain: "A push or pull can make an object speed up, slow down, change direction or change shape.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is a contact force?", options: ["Gravitational force", "Magnetic force", "Electrostatic force", "Friction"], answer: 3, explain: "Friction acts only when surfaces touch. Gravity, magnetic and electrostatic forces act at a distance.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The weight of an object is the force on it due to", options: ["friction", "gravity", "magnetism", "static charge"], answer: 1, explain: "Weight is the gravitational pull of the Earth on the object.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When the north poles of two magnets are brought close to each other, they", options: ["attract each other", "do not interact", "repel each other", "become unmagnetised"], answer: 2, explain: "Like poles repel and unlike poles attract.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Oil applied between moving parts of a machine", options: ["reduces friction", "increases the weight", "makes the parts stick together", "increases wear"], answer: 0, explain: "A lubricant forms a thin layer between surfaces and lowers friction.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The weight of a 5 kg bag on Earth (take g = 10 N/kg) is", options: ["50 N", "5 N", "500 N", "0.5 N"], answer: 0, explain: "Weight = mass × g = 5 × 10 = 50 N.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A force of 40 N acts to the east and a force of 25 N acts to the west on the same object. The net force is", options: ["65 N east", "15 N east", "15 N west", "25 N east"], answer: 1, explain: "Opposite forces subtract: 40 − 25 = 15 N, in the direction of the larger force, east.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A heavy box is hard to drag along the floor but easy to move after it is placed on a few round logs.\nWhy is this?", options: ["The logs reduce the weight of the box", "The logs increase the force of the push", "Gravity stops acting on the box", "Rolling friction is less than sliding friction"], answer: 3, explain: "On logs the box rolls instead of sliding, and rolling friction is much smaller than sliding friction.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): A comb rubbed on dry hair can pick up small bits of paper.\nReason (R): The charged comb exerts an electrostatic force on the paper.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Rubbing charges the comb, and the electrostatic force between the charged comb and the paper pulls the bits towards it.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Which pair contains only non-contact forces?", options: ["Friction and muscular force", "Gravitational force and magnetic force", "Muscular force and gravitational force", "Friction and electrostatic force"], answer: 1, explain: "Gravitational and magnetic forces act without touching. Friction and muscular force need contact.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In a tug of war, team A pulls with 500 N and team B pulls with 450 N in the opposite direction. What happens?", options: ["The rope moves towards B with a net force of 50 N", "The rope moves towards A with a net force of 50 N", "The rope does not move because the forces are in a tug of war", "The rope moves towards A with a net force of 950 N"], answer: 1, explain: "The forces are unbalanced. Net force = 500 − 450 = 50 N, towards the stronger team A.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Two children push a box in the same direction with 30 N and 20 N. Friction of 15 N acts against the motion. The net force on the box is", options: ["65 N", "5 N", "35 N", "15 N"], answer: 2, explain: "Pushes add: 30 + 20 = 50 N. Subtracting friction: 50 − 15 = 35 N in the direction of the push.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which arrangement shows the three types of friction in increasing order of size?", options: ["Static, sliding, rolling", "Sliding, rolling, static", "Rolling, static, sliding", "Rolling, sliding, static"], answer: 3, explain: "Rolling friction is the smallest, sliding friction is larger and static friction is the largest.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Cricketers and footballers wear shoes with spikes or studs on grass.\nWhy are they useful?", options: ["They increase friction with the ground, so players do not slip", "They remove friction completely", "They reduce the weight of the player", "They stop gravity from pulling the player down"], answer: 0, explain: "Spikes dig into the ground and increase friction, which helps the player to start, stop and turn without slipping.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define friction.", a: "Friction is a force that opposes the relative motion of two surfaces in contact.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What happens when two like charges are brought near each other?", a: "They repel each other (unlike charges attract).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Differentiate between balanced and unbalanced forces.", a: "Balanced forces have a net force of zero and do not change the state of motion of an object.\nUnbalanced forces have a net force that changes the speed or direction of the object.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Give two disadvantages of friction and two ways to reduce it.", a: "Disadvantages: it causes wear and tear of moving parts, and produces unwanted heat.\nWays to reduce it: use lubricants such as oil, polish the surfaces, or use wheels and ball bearings.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Calculate the weight of a 12 kg bag. Take g = 10 N/kg.", a: "Weight = mass × g.\n= 12 × 10 = 120 N.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Two children push a box in the same direction with forces of 25 N and 35 N. Friction of 20 N acts against them. Find the net force and say whether the box will move.", a: "Total push = 25 + 35 = 60 N.\nNet force = 60 − 20 = 40 N in the direction of the push.\nThe net force is not zero (unbalanced), so the box will move.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain with an example each how a force can change (a) the speed, (b) the direction and (c) the shape of an object.", a: "(a) Speed: a bowler's push makes a ball move faster, or brakes slow a cycle.\n(b) Direction: a hit with a bat changes the direction of a moving cricket ball.\n(c) Shape: pressing dough or squeezing a sponge changes its shape.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: In a tug of war, team A pulls the rope to the right with 800 N and team B pulls it to the left with 600 N.\n(i) Find the net force and its direction.\n(ii) What will happen to the rope?\n(iii) What will happen if team B also pulls with 800 N?", a: "(i) Net force = 800 − 600 = 200 N towards the right (towards team A).\n(ii) The forces are unbalanced, so the rope moves towards team A.\n(iii) The forces are balanced, the net force is 0 N, so the rope does not move.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "pressure-winds-storms-cyclones": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Pressure is equal to", options: ["force × area", "force ÷ area", "area ÷ force", "force + area"], answer: 1, explain: "Pressure is the force acting on a unit area, so P = F ÷ A.", level: 1 },
      { q: "One pascal is the same as", options: ["1 N·m", "1 N × m²", "1 N/m²", "1 J/s"], answer: 2, explain: "1 Pa = 1 N/m², a force of one newton on an area of one square metre.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In a liquid, the pressure increases with", options: ["depth", "the colour of the liquid", "the width of the container", "the shape of the container's lid"], answer: 0, explain: "The deeper we go, the greater the weight of liquid above, so the pressure increases with depth.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Warm air, compared with cold air, is", options: ["heavier and sinks", "denser", "unchanged", "lighter and rises"], answer: 3, explain: "Warm air is lighter and rises, leaving a region of low pressure.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Cyclones form over", options: ["warm tropical oceans", "cold polar land", "dry deserts", "mountain peaks"], answer: 0, explain: "Cyclones form over warm tropical oceans around a low-pressure centre.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A force of 300 N acts on an area of 0.6 m². The pressure is", options: ["180 Pa", "0.002 Pa", "500 Pa", "300.6 Pa"], answer: 2, explain: "P = F ÷ A = 300 ÷ 0.6 = 500 Pa.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Rohan carries a heavy bag. A thin strap cuts into his shoulder, but a wide strap does not.\nWhy?", options: ["The wide strap is lighter", "A thin strap increases gravity", "The wide strap makes the bag cooler", "The wide strap spreads the force over a larger area, so the pressure is less"], answer: 3, explain: "For the same force, pressure is less when the area is larger.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): We can sip a drink through a straw.\nReason (R): Sucking lowers the pressure inside the straw, and the higher atmospheric pressure on the liquid surface pushes the liquid up.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "The difference in pressure between the open surface of the drink and the inside of the straw pushes the liquid up, so R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "A low-pressure area forms over the sea. Winds near it will blow", options: ["away from the low-pressure area", "from the surrounding higher-pressure regions towards the low-pressure area", "only upwards", "in no particular direction"], answer: 1, explain: "Air moves from high pressure to low pressure, so winds blow towards the low-pressure area.", level: 2 },
      { q: "During a thunderstorm Kabir is playing in an open field with a few tall trees nearby.\nWhat is the safest thing to do?", options: ["Move into a closed building", "Stand under a tall tree", "Stand in the open field holding a metal rod", "Lie down in the open field"], answer: 0, explain: "During a thunderstorm we should go indoors and keep away from tall trees and electric poles.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A boy of weight 600 N stands on both feet. The total area of contact of his shoes with the ground is 0.03 m². The pressure on the ground is", options: ["200 Pa", "6 000 Pa", "18 000 Pa", "20 000 Pa"], answer: 3, explain: "P = F ÷ A = 600 ÷ 0.03 = 20 000 Pa.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A block weighing 400 N measures 2 m × 0.5 m × 0.25 m. The pressure on the ground when it rests on its smallest face is", options: ["400 Pa", "800 Pa", "3200 Pa", "1600 Pa"], answer: 2, explain: "The smallest face is 0.5 m × 0.25 m = 0.125 m². P = 400 ÷ 0.125 = 3200 Pa. (On the largest face, 1 m², it would be only 400 Pa.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "Why does a land breeze blow from the land to the sea at night?", options: ["The land is warmer than the sea at night", "The sea is colder than the land at night", "The land cools faster than the sea, so the air over the sea is warmer and rises, and air moves in from the land", "The wind blows from low pressure over the land to high pressure over the sea"], answer: 2, explain: "At night the land cools faster. Air above the warmer sea rises and creates low pressure, and cooler air from the land flows towards it.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which sequence correctly describes the formation of a thunderstorm?", options: ["Cold dry air sinks → no clouds form → light drizzle", "Clouds form → warm air sinks → the sky clears", "Heavy rain falls → warm moist air rises → clouds form", "Warm moist air rises quickly → clouds form → heavy rain, lightning and thunder"], answer: 3, explain: "In a thunderstorm warm moist air rises quickly and forms clouds, which give heavy rain, lightning and thunder.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define atmospheric pressure.", a: "Atmospheric pressure is the pressure exerted by the weight of the air above us.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why do camels have wide, flat feet?", a: "Wide feet have a large area, so the pressure on the sand is small and the camel does not sink.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why does a sharp knife cut better than a blunt knife when the same force is applied?", a: "The sharp edge has a very small area, so the same force produces a large pressure.\nThe large pressure cuts the object easily.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A force of 900 N acts on an area of 1.5 m². Calculate the pressure.", a: "P = F ÷ A.\n= 900 ÷ 1.5 = 600 Pa.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain how winds are produced. Give two points.", a: "Air over a heated region becomes warm, lighter and rises, creating a low-pressure region.\nAir from a nearby high-pressure region moves in to take its place; this moving air is wind.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A wooden block weighing 400 N measures 2 m × 0.5 m × 0.25 m. Find the maximum and the minimum pressure it can exert on the ground.", a: "Smallest face = 0.5 × 0.25 = 0.125 m², largest face = 2 × 0.5 = 1 m².\nMaximum pressure (on the smallest face) = 400 ÷ 0.125 = 3200 Pa.\nMinimum pressure (on the largest face) = 400 ÷ 1 = 400 Pa.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how a land breeze is formed at night.", a: "At night the land cools faster than the sea, so the air over the sea is warmer than the air over the land.\nThe warm air over the sea rises, forming a low-pressure region.\nCooler air from the land moves towards the sea to take its place, so the breeze blows from land to sea.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: The India Meteorological Department tracks a storm over a warm part of the sea with satellites. Strong winds spiral around a low-pressure centre, and heavy rain is expected on the coast. Villagers are asked to move to shelters.\n(i) Name this kind of storm.\n(ii) What is the low-pressure centre of this storm called?\n(iii) Give two precautions the villagers should take.", a: "(i) A cyclone.\n(ii) The eye.\n(iii) Any two: follow official warnings and move to a shelter, stay indoors, keep away from tall trees and electric poles.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "particulate-nature-of-matter": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which state of matter has a fixed shape and a fixed volume?", options: ["Gas", "Solid", "Liquid", "Steam"], answer: 1, explain: "A solid has a fixed shape and volume. A liquid has a fixed volume only and a gas has neither.", level: 1 },
      { q: "According to the particle model, matter is made of", options: ["a continuous material with no gaps", "only liquids", "tiny particles with spaces between them", "a single solid block"], answer: 2, explain: "Matter consists of very small particles that have spaces between them and are in constant motion.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The change of a liquid into vapour from its surface at any temperature is called", options: ["evaporation", "freezing", "sublimation", "condensation"], answer: 0, explain: "Evaporation happens at the surface and at any temperature. Boiling happens at the boiling point throughout the liquid.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The melting point of ice is", options: ["100 °C", "37 °C", "−10 °C", "0 °C"], answer: 3, explain: "Ice melts at 0 °C. 100 °C is the boiling point of water at normal pressure.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Water droplets that form on the outside of a glass of cold water are formed by", options: ["boiling", "sublimation", "condensation", "melting"], answer: 2, explain: "Water vapour in the air loses heat on touching the cold glass and condenses into droplets.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In which of these is the force of attraction between particles the weakest?", options: ["Steam", "Ice", "Water", "Iron"], answer: 0, explain: "In a gas such as steam the particles are far apart and the attraction is weakest.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A gas fills the whole container in which it is kept because", options: ["gas particles are closely packed", "gas particles move freely and are far apart", "gas particles do not move", "gas particles are heavier than liquid particles"], answer: 1, explain: "Gas particles move freely in all directions and spread out until they fill the container.", level: 2 },
      { q: "After a bath Meera stands in a breeze and feels cold.\nWhat is the reason?", options: ["Air is made of cold particles", "Water on her skin freezes", "The towel takes away all the heat", "Water on her skin evaporates by taking heat from her body"], answer: 3, explain: "Evaporation needs heat, which the water takes from the body, so the body feels cooler.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): Solids cannot be compressed easily.\nReason (R): The particles of a solid are far apart.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 2, explain: "Solids cannot be compressed because their particles are very closely packed, so R is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Diffusion takes place fastest in", options: ["solids", "liquids", "all three states equally", "gases"], answer: 3, explain: "Particles of a gas move freely and have large spaces between them, so diffusion is fastest in gases.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A spoonful of sugar dissolves in a full glass of water without making the water overflow. What does this suggest?", options: ["Sugar particles disappear completely", "Sugar particles fit into the spaces between water particles", "Water particles become smaller", "Sugar changes into a gas"], answer: 1, explain: "There are spaces between water particles, and the sugar particles move into them.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A student heats ice and records its temperature each minute: −5 °C, −2 °C, 0 °C, 0 °C, 0 °C, 3 °C, 8 °C. What is happening during the three readings of 0 °C?", options: ["The heater has stopped working", "The water is boiling", "The ice is melting, and the heat is used to overcome the attraction between particles", "The particles have stopped moving"], answer: 2, explain: "During a change of state the temperature stays constant because the heat energy is used to loosen the attraction between particles.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which arrangement shows iron, water and oxygen gas in increasing order of the spaces between their particles?", options: ["Water, iron, oxygen", "Oxygen, water, iron", "Iron, oxygen, water", "Iron, water, oxygen"], answer: 3, explain: "Solid iron has the smallest spaces, liquid water larger spaces and oxygen gas the largest.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Water in an earthen pot (matka) stays cool in summer. A little water seeps through tiny pores to the outside of the pot.\nWhat explains this?", options: ["Clay is always colder than water", "The seeped water evaporates from the outer surface, taking heat from the pot and the water inside", "The water inside is freezing slowly", "Heat cannot enter a clay pot at all"], answer: 1, explain: "Evaporation takes heat from the surroundings, so the pot and the water in it are cooled.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define diffusion.", a: "Diffusion is the mixing of particles of one substance into another by their own motion.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the melting point of ice and the boiling point of water at normal pressure.", a: "The melting point of ice is 0 °C and the boiling point of water is 100 °C.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give two examples that show particles of matter are in constant motion.", a: "The smell of incense or food spreads through a room (diffusion in air).\nInk spreads through water on its own (diffusion in a liquid).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Compare a solid and a liquid on the basis of shape, volume and the movement of particles.", a: "A solid has a fixed shape and fixed volume, and its particles only vibrate in place.\nA liquid has a fixed volume but takes the shape of its container, and its particles slide past one another.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why does sugar dissolve faster in hot water than in cold water?", a: "Particles move faster at a higher temperature.\nSo the particles of sugar and water mix more quickly, which makes diffusion faster.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe the three states of matter in terms of arrangement of particles, force of attraction and compressibility.", a: "Solid: particles closely packed, strongest attraction, cannot be compressed easily.\nLiquid: particles close but able to slide, weaker attraction, hardly compressible.\nGas: particles far apart, weakest attraction, easily compressed.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Ice is heated steadily. What happens to its temperature while it melts at 0 °C? Explain using the particle model.", a: "The temperature stays constant at 0 °C until all the ice has melted.\nThe heat energy is not used to raise the temperature.\nIt is used to overcome the force of attraction between the particles, so that they can slide past each other as a liquid.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: Anita lights an incense stick in one corner of a room. After a few minutes her friend sitting at the other corner smells it. In summer the smell reaches the other corner sooner than in winter.\n(i) Name the process by which the smell spreads.\n(ii) Why is it faster in summer?\n(iii) In which state of matter is this process the fastest?", a: "(i) Diffusion.\n(ii) At a higher temperature the particles move faster, so they mix more quickly.\n(iii) In gases.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
